[[:db/retract 17592186124763 :question/answer-fields 17592186121175] [:db/retract 17592186121169 :answer-field/choices 17592186121170] [:db/retract 17592186121169 :answer-field/choices 17592186121171] [:db/retract 17592186121169 :answer-field/choices 17592186121172] [:db/retract 17592186121169 :answer-field/choices 17592186121173] [:db/retract 17592186121169 :answer-field/choices 17592186121174] [:db/retract 17592186121193 :answer-field/choices 17592186121194] [:db/retract 17592186121193 :answer-field/choices 17592186121195] [:db/retract 17592186121193 :answer-field/choices 17592186121196] [:db/retract 17592186121193 :answer-field/choices 17592186121197] [:db/retract 17592186121193 :answer-field/choices 17592186121198] {:db/id 17592186124673 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d00e40d-1dde-5d1e-965b-4da23aadf13a" "question-9240a0d5-9357-59a5-be58-f932d93ba4fa" "question-6a1e7865-6d9a-51ad-a7cc-78b26889323e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124373 :question/problem "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n\nIV. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-531996ff-0ee7-5943-af5b-93c7fc2f947e" "question-4108ce6a-5d28-540e-b8c0-e4b52161a458"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-54a5418f-f9b3-5d4d-9779-13b8cf5ffccc" "question-490a86ce-e0bc-535b-a5a3-da17f507f78c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d00e40d-1dde-5d1e-965b-4da23aadf13a" :question/id #uuid "9d00e40d-1dde-5d1e-965b-4da23aadf13a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37332" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=638-0.02P,\\,P(0)=31\\,200,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=638-0.02P=0.02(31\\,900-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.02,a=31\\,900,$ and ${y}_{0}=31\\,200.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,216-8P=8(277-P),\\,P(0)=291,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=26-0.005P=0.005(5\\,200-P),\\,P(0)=5\\,600\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=291>a=277$ and ${y}_{0}=5\\,600>a=5\\,200.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-074e4dbe-f5ba-52f9-92ce-1e5707e85955"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-074e4dbe-f5ba-52f9-92ce-1e5707e85955" :answer-field/id #uuid "074e4dbe-f5ba-52f9-92ce-1e5707e85955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a5bed931-7bc1-5eaf-b7ae-e65ed3f624aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0e58d094-5b17-5bdb-b3cc-ac9ced48cf12" "answer-9e8720f5-820e-5963-b078-aeb673451966" "answer-f7934c18-cba3-5f1e-b7f5-2378dd7ae5b4" "answer-fb732190-8be4-59f5-8e54-9b8dba6f50df" "answer-a5bed931-7bc1-5eaf-b7ae-e65ed3f624aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e58d094-5b17-5bdb-b3cc-ac9ced48cf12" :answer/id #uuid "0e58d094-5b17-5bdb-b3cc-ac9ced48cf12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.85P-346,\\,P(0)=451.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e8720f5-820e-5963-b078-aeb673451966" :answer/id #uuid "9e8720f5-820e-5963-b078-aeb673451966" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=26-0.005P,\\,P(0)=5\\,600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7934c18-cba3-5f1e-b7f5-2378dd7ae5b4" :answer/id #uuid "f7934c18-cba3-5f1e-b7f5-2378dd7ae5b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,216-8P,\\,P(0)=291" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb732190-8be4-59f5-8e54-9b8dba6f50df" :answer/id #uuid "fb732190-8be4-59f5-8e54-9b8dba6f50df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=436P-139\\,956,\\,P(0)=165\\,143" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5bed931-7bc1-5eaf-b7ae-e65ed3f624aa" :answer/id #uuid "a5bed931-7bc1-5eaf-b7ae-e65ed3f624aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=638-0.02P,\\,P(0)=31\\,200" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124762 :question/problem "Given that $y<150$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5(150-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.5,$ and $a=150.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<150$ for all $t\\ge 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121169 :answer-field/correct "answer-2c41e7d9-f7c3-59f1-82a1-92abfdfa62ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7e94fce8-561e-540e-9a23-0e72db5154a0" "answer-2c41e7d9-f7c3-59f1-82a1-92abfdfa62ba" "answer-4611a9e7-a8cc-5133-9e08-255ac5b7a2fc" "answer-5853d9b3-2ca6-58cf-bf5c-4c5d47d3fa61" "answer-fa740537-bee8-5451-a7ef-49020d73940b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e94fce8-561e-540e-9a23-0e72db5154a0" :answer/id #uuid "7e94fce8-561e-540e-9a23-0e72db5154a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-0.5(y+150)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c41e7d9-f7c3-59f1-82a1-92abfdfa62ba" :answer/id #uuid "2c41e7d9-f7c3-59f1-82a1-92abfdfa62ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5(150-y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4611a9e7-a8cc-5133-9e08-255ac5b7a2fc" :answer/id #uuid "4611a9e7-a8cc-5133-9e08-255ac5b7a2fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5(y-150)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5853d9b3-2ca6-58cf-bf5c-4c5d47d3fa61" :answer/id #uuid "5853d9b3-2ca6-58cf-bf5c-4c5d47d3fa61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=150(y+0.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa740537-bee8-5451-a7ef-49020d73940b" :answer/id #uuid "fa740537-bee8-5451-a7ef-49020d73940b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=150(y-0.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9240a0d5-9357-59a5-be58-f932d93ba4fa" :question/id #uuid "9240a0d5-9357-59a5-be58-f932d93ba4fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-335028" :question/problem "Given that $y<200$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.3(200-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.3,$ and $a=200.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<200$ for all $t\\ge 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2b69f173-f151-5b59-a56f-2ab0651775c9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2b69f173-f151-5b59-a56f-2ab0651775c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b69f173-f151-5b59-a56f-2ab0651775c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b950d605-acf5-5f78-8ce1-1df43ba7eee9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-90bf424a-a5c7-51c0-9e49-9cf13b16339d" "answer-b950d605-acf5-5f78-8ce1-1df43ba7eee9" "answer-c636b844-1f99-53bf-a9e3-46fb747c0939" "answer-c44d4eaa-a7ce-5ab7-ae4e-afa08dff211b" "answer-d1448329-aba0-5e4c-a382-514f67f96729"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90bf424a-a5c7-51c0-9e49-9cf13b16339d" :answer/id #uuid "90bf424a-a5c7-51c0-9e49-9cf13b16339d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-0.3(y+200)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b950d605-acf5-5f78-8ce1-1df43ba7eee9" :answer/id #uuid "b950d605-acf5-5f78-8ce1-1df43ba7eee9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.3(200-y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c636b844-1f99-53bf-a9e3-46fb747c0939" :answer/id #uuid "c636b844-1f99-53bf-a9e3-46fb747c0939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.3(y-200)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c44d4eaa-a7ce-5ab7-ae4e-afa08dff211b" :answer/id #uuid "c44d4eaa-a7ce-5ab7-ae4e-afa08dff211b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=200(y+0.3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1448329-aba0-5e4c-a382-514f67f96729" :answer/id #uuid "d1448329-aba0-5e4c-a382-514f67f96729" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=200(y-0.3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6a1e7865-6d9a-51ad-a7cc-78b26889323e" :question/id #uuid "6a1e7865-6d9a-51ad-a7cc-78b26889323e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15593" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=1-0.000\\,05P,\\,P(0)=10\\,000,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=1-0.000\\,05P=0.000\\,05(20\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.000\\,05,a=20\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=10\\,000.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=3-0.000\\,4P=0.000\\,4(7,500-P),\\,P(0)=13\\,000,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=5-0.005P=0.005(1\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=2\\,000\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=13\\,000>a=7\\,500,$ and ${y}_{0}=2\\,000>a=1\\,000.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a035cca5-87cc-5312-bfcd-68835b076102"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a035cca5-87cc-5312-bfcd-68835b076102" :answer-field/id #uuid "a035cca5-87cc-5312-bfcd-68835b076102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-26f02fa5-9d0e-5303-a3ab-ebbe45c96c17" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1d3b80cf-1624-511b-9f84-0dbd23445796" "answer-e7ae9662-9561-5fab-8fd8-0e95098b9508" "answer-26f02fa5-9d0e-5303-a3ab-ebbe45c96c17" "answer-e677655c-880b-5a45-8220-b2709155e399" "answer-ec8066cc-27f1-54d2-868f-a05b90f194ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d3b80cf-1624-511b-9f84-0dbd23445796" :answer/id #uuid "1d3b80cf-1624-511b-9f84-0dbd23445796" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.001P-25,\\,P(0)=3\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7ae9662-9561-5fab-8fd8-0e95098b9508" :answer/id #uuid "e7ae9662-9561-5fab-8fd8-0e95098b9508" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P-225,\\,P(0)=1\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26f02fa5-9d0e-5303-a3ab-ebbe45c96c17" :answer/id #uuid "26f02fa5-9d0e-5303-a3ab-ebbe45c96c17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=1-0.000\\,05P,\\,P(0)=10\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e677655c-880b-5a45-8220-b2709155e399" :answer/id #uuid "e677655c-880b-5a45-8220-b2709155e399" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=3-0.000\\,4P,\\,P(0)=13\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec8066cc-27f1-54d2-868f-a05b90f194ef" :answer/id #uuid "ec8066cc-27f1-54d2-868f-a05b90f194ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=5-0.005P,\\,P(0)=2\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-531996ff-0ee7-5943-af5b-93c7fc2f947e" :question/id #uuid "531996ff-0ee7-5943-af5b-93c7fc2f947e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335144" :question/problem "A bird sanctuary initially has $80$ birds. The number of birds in the sanctuary $B(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the sanctuary is $110$ birds, which differential equation could be used to model the bird population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of birds in the sanctuary obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of birds changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of birds in the sanctuary. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the bird sanctuary, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=110$ birds, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(110-B).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-49c71e39-1e77-56e6-92ec-6a20e0c6c4b4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-49c71e39-1e77-56e6-92ec-6a20e0c6c4b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "49c71e39-1e77-56e6-92ec-6a20e0c6c4b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-10c45811-628c-5ba4-8e0a-f7d902d884a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10c45811-628c-5ba4-8e0a-f7d902d884a9" "answer-83fdb903-a322-5109-85eb-f06a871c051d" "answer-353017b2-4607-5104-afdb-932463c44124" "answer-207c4468-291f-59e8-87bb-de8bc6fba242" "answer-c25a8b30-27a2-57da-864d-b562f2ea668b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10c45811-628c-5ba4-8e0a-f7d902d884a9" :answer/id #uuid "10c45811-628c-5ba4-8e0a-f7d902d884a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(110-B)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83fdb903-a322-5109-85eb-f06a871c051d" :answer/id #uuid "83fdb903-a322-5109-85eb-f06a871c051d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(190-B)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-353017b2-4607-5104-afdb-932463c44124" :answer/id #uuid "353017b2-4607-5104-afdb-932463c44124" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(280-B)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-207c4468-291f-59e8-87bb-de8bc6fba242" :answer/id #uuid "207c4468-291f-59e8-87bb-de8bc6fba242" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(30-B)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c25a8b30-27a2-57da-864d-b562f2ea668b" :answer/id #uuid "c25a8b30-27a2-57da-864d-b562f2ea668b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(70-B)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124766 :question/problem "A factory's management noted that the growth of the unit production is directly proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Let $P(t)$ be the production quantity, measured in units produced, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in days. If the maximum number of units that a worker can produce is $70$ $\\text{units/day}$, which differential equation could be used to model the factory's production? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the production $P(t)$ of the factory changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=70\\text{ units/day},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(70-P).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121193 :answer-field/correct "answer-7fe52287-4fa4-5c84-ad29-268c6a5296c3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ecf94966-8385-545a-b21e-358160ff193e" "answer-f700ef32-7ece-558e-a620-e91790aaa0bb" "answer-7fe52287-4fa4-5c84-ad29-268c6a5296c3" "answer-05b74483-29bf-585d-92c8-7ccb3f928a45" "answer-993db06a-cd74-5472-8bca-5491bd9aed54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecf94966-8385-545a-b21e-358160ff193e" :answer/id #uuid "ecf94966-8385-545a-b21e-358160ff193e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(70+P)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f700ef32-7ece-558e-a620-e91790aaa0bb" :answer/id #uuid "f700ef32-7ece-558e-a620-e91790aaa0bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(70-P)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fe52287-4fa4-5c84-ad29-268c6a5296c3" :answer/id #uuid "7fe52287-4fa4-5c84-ad29-268c6a5296c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(70-P)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05b74483-29bf-585d-92c8-7ccb3f928a45" :answer/id #uuid "05b74483-29bf-585d-92c8-7ccb3f928a45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=kP(70-P)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-993db06a-cd74-5472-8bca-5491bd9aed54" :answer/id #uuid "993db06a-cd74-5472-8bca-5491bd9aed54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k{P}^{2}(70-P)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4108ce6a-5d28-540e-b8c0-e4b52161a458" :question/id #uuid "4108ce6a-5d28-540e-b8c0-e4b52161a458" :question/math-academy-id "q-344232" :question/problem "A parking garage initially contains $70$ vehicles. The number of vehicles in the garage $V(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in hours. If the maximum capacity of the garage is $250$ vehicles, which differential equation could be used to model the number of vehicles in the garage? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of vehicles in the garage obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of vehicles changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of vehicles in the garage. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-V),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the garage, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=250$ vehicles, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(250-V).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d06901e9-40fe-5fc7-ae69-f932c626e4ea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d06901e9-40fe-5fc7-ae69-f932c626e4ea" :answer-field/id #uuid "d06901e9-40fe-5fc7-ae69-f932c626e4ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d0d96262-4712-58ed-b608-ce9c6dab0eee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-043dc448-2d64-5bd5-b613-9351f25fcbe7" "answer-d0d96262-4712-58ed-b608-ce9c6dab0eee" "answer-a61a1a66-20a6-5535-8610-2d133482e2d8" "answer-b18a7bfd-9662-5019-81d6-5d34da173873" "answer-bb01bcbc-7723-5c54-bf42-c8d5e8f3b897"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-043dc448-2d64-5bd5-b613-9351f25fcbe7" :answer/id #uuid "043dc448-2d64-5bd5-b613-9351f25fcbe7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(120-V)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0d96262-4712-58ed-b608-ce9c6dab0eee" :answer/id #uuid "d0d96262-4712-58ed-b608-ce9c6dab0eee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(250-V)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a61a1a66-20a6-5535-8610-2d133482e2d8" :answer/id #uuid "a61a1a66-20a6-5535-8610-2d133482e2d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(300-V)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b18a7bfd-9662-5019-81d6-5d34da173873" :answer/id #uuid "b18a7bfd-9662-5019-81d6-5d34da173873" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(500-V)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb01bcbc-7723-5c54-bf42-c8d5e8f3b897" :answer/id #uuid "bb01bcbc-7723-5c54-bf42-c8d5e8f3b897" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(70-V)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125068 :question/problem "A poultry farmer has egg-laying chickens. She knows that her farm can produce a maximum of $500$ eggs a day; however, it is far behind it.\nSince she started monitoring, the chickens laid $390$ eggs and $400$ eggs on days $3$ and $6,$ respectively. How many eggs do the chickens lay on day $42?$\nRound your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the egg production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the egg production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible egg production and its current egg production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the farm, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=500$ eggs, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(500-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{500-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{500-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|500-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $500-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}500-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 500-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 500-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the egg production was $390$ eggs. So, applying the condition $E(3)=390,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(3) & =390 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-3k} & =390 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =110.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $6,$ the egg production was $400$ eggs. So, applying the condition $E(6)=400,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(6) & =400 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-6k} & =400 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-6k}} & =\\frac{110}{100} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{11}{10} \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{11}{10}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{11}{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)} & =110 \\\\ K & =110{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)} \\\\ K & =121\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=500-121{e}^{-(t/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of eggs laid on day $t=42$ is\n\n$$\nE(42)=500-121{e}^{-(42/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)}≈468\\,\\text{eggs},\n$$\n\n rounded to nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123008 :answer-field/correct 17592186123009 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-54a5418f-f9b3-5d4d-9779-13b8cf5ffccc" :question/id #uuid "54a5418f-f9b3-5d4d-9779-13b8cf5ffccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335644" :question/problem "A coastal research station is monitoring the population of brine shrimp in a controlled seawater tank. The tank can sustain a maximum population of $500$ shrimp, but the population is still below this limit. Since researchers began tracking the growth, the population reached $452$ shrimp on day $3$ and $473$ shrimp on day $9.$ On what day will the population reach $490$ shrimp? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population of shrimp, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the habitat can sustain, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=500$ shrimp, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{500-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{500-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|500-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}500-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 500-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the population was $452$ shrimp. So, applying the condition $P(3)=452,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =452 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-3k} & =452 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the population was $473$ shrimp. So, applying the condition $P(9)=473,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =473 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-9k} & =473 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{48}{27} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16/9)} & =48 \\\\ K & =48{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16/9)} \\\\ K & =48\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\ K & =48\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ K & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=500-64{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16/9)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$ be the day when the population reaches $490$ shrimp. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =490 \\\\ 500-64{e}^{-kd} & =490 \\\\ 10 & =64{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{10}{64}=\\frac{5}{32} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{32}) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{32}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the population will be $490$ shrimp on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{32})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})}=19.3578…≈19,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fdf03520-5f9c-50d4-9411-5e12d6f34453"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fdf03520-5f9c-50d4-9411-5e12d6f34453" :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf03520-5f9c-50d4-9411-5e12d6f34453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-05356339-ef8c-55b3-b26e-1d5a396dcc30" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d3494c8-d836-50c1-838b-c0be8c2b5c15" "answer-8d11d60a-b68b-5f48-b3c9-db4c2154d10c" "answer-05356339-ef8c-55b3-b26e-1d5a396dcc30" "answer-cbc2a5b1-cdc4-5108-82ee-76eaa5f4a571" "answer-99473357-017e-59bf-827f-4097bb32b024"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d3494c8-d836-50c1-838b-c0be8c2b5c15" :answer/id #uuid "3d3494c8-d836-50c1-838b-c0be8c2b5c15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$15\\text{th}$ day" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d11d60a-b68b-5f48-b3c9-db4c2154d10c" :answer/id #uuid "8d11d60a-b68b-5f48-b3c9-db4c2154d10c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$17\\text{th}$ day" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05356339-ef8c-55b3-b26e-1d5a396dcc30" :answer/id #uuid "05356339-ef8c-55b3-b26e-1d5a396dcc30" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$19\\text{th}$ day" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbc2a5b1-cdc4-5108-82ee-76eaa5f4a571" :answer/id #uuid "cbc2a5b1-cdc4-5108-82ee-76eaa5f4a571" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$22\\text{th}$ day" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99473357-017e-59bf-827f-4097bb32b024" :answer/id #uuid "99473357-017e-59bf-827f-4097bb32b024" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ day" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-490a86ce-e0bc-535b-a5a3-da17f507f78c" :question/id #uuid "490a86ce-e0bc-535b-a5a3-da17f507f78c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335599" :question/problem "A high-speed printer in an office building has a maximum printing capacity of $640$ pages per minute; however, current production is below this maximum. Since the technician started monitoring, the printer produced $480$ and $560$ pages per minute at minutes $6$ and $12,$ respectively. What is the printer's printing rate at minute $36?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the printing production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in minutes.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the printing production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible printing production and its current printing production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the printer, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=640$ pages, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(640-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{640-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{640-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|640-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $640-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}640-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 640-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 640-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =640-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, at minute $6,$ the printing production was $480$ pages per minute. So, applying the condition $E(6)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(6) & =480 \\\\ 640-K{e}^{-6k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =160.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, at minute $12,$ the printing production was $560$ pages per minute. So, applying the condition $E(12)=560,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(12) & =560 \\\\ 640-K{e}^{-12k} & =560 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-6k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{160}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =2 \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-6\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} & =160 \\\\ K & =160{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\ K & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=640-320{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of pages printed per minute at minute $t=36$ is\n\n$$\nE(36)=640-320{e}^{-(36/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}=635\\,\\text{pages per minute}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a75644c-e1e3-5402-a410-adaf11ab9750"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a75644c-e1e3-5402-a410-adaf11ab9750" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a75644c-e1e3-5402-a410-adaf11ab9750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-079c9887-f4cd-5533-8644-4c4f30e866d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80e6d145-65a0-5b9c-af2c-46945fb281ff" "answer-789ebe1b-2dd1-5b2f-9475-7f0a0488da18" "answer-7048ec9a-91f3-5e70-89ed-a45fb3e31905" "answer-079c9887-f4cd-5533-8644-4c4f30e866d1" "answer-d123b220-d31c-5343-8d01-36cf77ecb21f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80e6d145-65a0-5b9c-af2c-46945fb281ff" :answer/id #uuid "80e6d145-65a0-5b9c-af2c-46945fb281ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$612$ pages" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-789ebe1b-2dd1-5b2f-9475-7f0a0488da18" :answer/id #uuid "789ebe1b-2dd1-5b2f-9475-7f0a0488da18" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$620$ pages" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7048ec9a-91f3-5e70-89ed-a45fb3e31905" :answer/id #uuid "7048ec9a-91f3-5e70-89ed-a45fb3e31905" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$628$ pages" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-079c9887-f4cd-5533-8644-4c4f30e866d1" :answer/id #uuid "079c9887-f4cd-5533-8644-4c4f30e866d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$635$ pages" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d123b220-d31c-5343-8d01-36cf77ecb21f" :answer/id #uuid "d123b220-d31c-5343-8d01-36cf77ecb21f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$640$ pages" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125811 :tutorial/content "In many real situations, a quantity increases quickly at first, but then the increase slows down and the quantity levels off near some fixed value.\n\nFor example, suppose a cold drink is taken out of a refrigerator and placed in a warm room. At first, its temperature rises rapidly because the difference between the drink's temperature and the room temperature is large. As the drink warms up, this difference decreases, and the warming slows down. Eventually, the temperature levels off and approaches the room temperature.\n\nThis type of behavior is described by an **inhibited growth model**.\n\nAn *inhibited growth equation* models situations where the rate at which a quantity $P$ increases depends on how far it is from a target value $a$, called the **equilibrium value**. This equilibrium value represents the level that the quantity approaches over time.\n\nAn inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = r(a-P),\n$$\n\nand an inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = r(a-P), \\qquad P(0) = P_0\n$$\n\nwhere $r > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $P < a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nIn this model, the equilibrium value $a$ acts as an upper bound. The solution increases over time but never rises above $a$, instead approaching it more and more slowly. The figure below illustrates this behavior.\n\n![](images/81/815441d6991917ade1594f1c60a90e9505577917b1088cae3b10c637b4486c15.png)\n\n**Note:** Inhibited growth and inhibited decay use the same basic idea: the rate of change is proportional to the difference between $P$ and the equilibrium value $a$. The only difference is whether the solution approaches $a$ from below (growth) or from above (decay).\n\nInhibited growth models can be written in expanded form. To identify an expanded equation as inhibited growth, we factorise out the *positive* coefficient of $P.$ For example, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 60-12P,\n$$\n\n*is* an inhibited growth equation for $P < 5$ and $t \\geq 0,$ since it can be expressed with $r=12$ and $a=5$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 12(5-P).\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125810 :tutorial/content "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited growth models, let’s work through an example to see how they are used in practice.\n\nSuppose a wildlife reserve is tracking the population of a reintroduced wolf pack. The environment can support a maximum population of $450$ wolves, but the current population is still below this limit. Since monitoring began, the population reached $392$ wolves after $6$ months and $420$ wolves after $12$ months.\n\nLet's construct an inhibited growth model to describe the wolf population in terms of the month $t.$\n\nThe rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the reserve can sustain, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=450,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k(450-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{450-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{450-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|450-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $450 - P > 0$ for all $t \\geq 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}450-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 450-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 450-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =450-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nThis is the general solution. On the next slide, we will use the population data to find the specific values for the constants $K$ and $k.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125711 :tutorial/content "Our general solution for the wolf population in month $t$ is $P(t) = 450 - Ke^{-kt}.$ Now, let's find the specific values of our constants.\n\nWe're told that, in month $6,$ the population was $392.$ So, applying the condition $P(6) = 392,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6) & =392 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-6k} & =392 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, in month $12,$ the population was $420.$ So, applying the condition $P(12) = 420,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =420 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-12k} & =420 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-6k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{58}{30} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{29}{15} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{29}{15}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{29}{15}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =58{e}^{6k} \\\\  & =58(\\frac{29}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1682}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the wolf population in month $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 450 - \\frac{1682}{15}e^{-(t/6)\\ln(29/15)}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124718 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
